= Ontology =

== Overview ==
'''Ontology''' has two major related meanings.

In philosophy, ontology studies what exists and the most general kinds, structures and relations of what exists.

In information science and artificial intelligence, an ontology is an explicit, often formal specification of a conceptualization or domain vocabulary used to represent entities, categories, properties, relations and constraints.

These senses influence each other but should not be treated as identical.

== Philosophical ontology ==
Philosophical ontology asks questions such as:

* What exists?
* What kinds of things exist?
* Are properties or relations entities?
* What makes an object the same object over time?
* Are events, processes, numbers or fictional objects real?
* Which distinctions are fundamental?

Ontology is usually treated as a part of metaphysics.

It also studies ontological commitment: what entities a theory or language commits us to accepting.

== Information-science ontology ==
In knowledge engineering, an ontology makes a conceptual model explicit enough to share and reuse.

An ontology can define:

* classes or categories;
* [[Entity|entities]] or individuals;
* [[Relation|relations]];
* [[Property|properties]];
* definitions;
* domain/range restrictions;
* equivalence/disjointness;
* axioms;
* constraints or rules, depending on the formalism.

Thomas Gruber's influential 1993 work described an ontology as a specification of a conceptualization for a shared domain of discourse.

== Conceptualization ==
A conceptualization is an abstract view of a domain: what kinds of things are relevant and how they can relate.

The ontology is a representation/specification of that view.

This gives an important separation:

<pre>
domain
≠ conceptualization
≠ ontology document
≠ ontology language
≠ serialization
</pre>

The same conceptualization can be encoded using different languages.

== Domain and top-level ontologies ==
A '''domain ontology''' models concepts and relations for a particular subject area such as medicine, finance or materials science.

A '''top-level ontology''' defines highly general categories intended to support interoperability across domains.

ISO/IEC 21838 specifies requirements and examples for top-level ontologies.

A top-level ontology does not replace domain knowledge; it provides general organizing distinctions with which domain ontologies can align.

== Ontology and taxonomy ==
A [[Taxonomy]] usually organizes concepts or categories, often through broader–narrower or parent–child hierarchy.

An ontology can include taxonomy but normally expresses richer semantics such as:
* multiple relation types;
* constraints;
* identity;
* disjointness;
* property characteristics;
* logical axioms.

A taxonomy can therefore be part of an ontology without being equivalent to an ontology.

== Ontology and schema ==
A [[Schema]] specifies expected structure.

Some schemas and ontologies overlap in practice, but their primary purposes often differ.

A database schema commonly constrains how records are stored.

An ontology commonly describes the meaning and relationships of represented domain concepts.

RDF Schema is an important boundary case: despite its name, it provides semantic vocabulary constructs and entailments rather than ordinary closed-world record validation.

== Ontology languages ==
An '''ontology language''' supplies syntax and formal semantics for writing ontologies.

Examples include:
* Common Logic;
* frame languages;
* [[Web Ontology Language|OWL]];
* description-logic-based formalisms.

An ontology is the content expressed in such a language; the language itself is not the ontology.

== Ontology engineering ==
Ontology engineering includes:

* scoping;
* competency questions;
* term/identifier selection;
* class/relation design;
* reuse and alignment;
* axiom design;
* consistency checking;
* documentation;
* versioning;
* governance.

Over-modeling can be as damaging as under-modeling. A distinction should justify its cost through improved meaning, interoperability, queries or reasoning.

== Ontology alignment ==
Different ontologies may model related domains using different assumptions.

Alignment can state:
* exact equivalence;
* broader/narrower mappings;
* related concepts;
* class/property correspondences;
* transformation rules.

Similarity of labels is not enough to justify equivalence.

== Open-world reasoning ==
Many ontology languages such as OWL operate under an open-world assumption.

Failure to assert a fact does not imply that the fact is false.

This differs from many database-validation contexts where missing required data can constitute failure.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki has a project-specific architecture at [[BITwiki:Ontology]] describing its entity types, relationships and semantic properties.

That is an information-science ontology application. It is not the definition of ontology itself and does not settle philosophical ontology.

